PR #148  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Spec reorg demotes instruction-fetch check, per-entry suppression, and non-priority entries from inline baseline register definitions to optional extensions; software must check HWCFG2 capability bits before relying on these features.
  [RISK] docs/domain_support.md:6-7  N/A (top-level documentation)
      Documentation lists IOPMP as a peer isolation primitive alongside PMP/ePMP without noting that IOPMP instruction-fetch enforcement (chk_x), per-entry error suppression (siwe/sere), and non-priority matching are extension-gated behind HWCFG2 capability bits in the reorganized spec. Software developers reading this doc as the entry point will assume all IOPMP features are baseline-mandatory, creating a design-level assumption that propagates into implementation.
  [RISK] include/sbi/sbi_hart_protection.h:16-39  N/A (struct sbi_hart_protection definition)
      The pluggable protection interface defines configure/unconfigure/map_range/unmap_range callbacks but has no capability-inquiry callback (e.g., a query_hwcfg or supports_chk_x method). Callers of sbi_hart_protection_best() or sbi_hart_protection_configure() cannot distinguish an IOPMP implementation with full DEP/sire/siwe/sere/sewe enforcement from one lacking instruction-fetch checking. The rating field is the only differentiator and it encodes no capability semantics. Without a capability-inquiry hook, any IOPMP backend registered here will be treated as functionally equivalent to PMP, silently dropping instruction-fetch protection on hardware that doesn't implement the chk_x extension.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:220-271  sbi_domain_check_addr
      This function performs CPU-side rwx permission checking against domain memory regions. It has no concept of I/O agent identity (SRCID), no distinction between CPU instruction fetch and I/O agent instruction fetch, and no per-entry error suppression logic (siwe/sere). Currently it is only invoked from CPU-side trap handlers (illegal instruction, load/store faults), so it is safe in its present usage. However, the function signature (addr + mode + access_flags with EXECUTE bit) is structurally reusable for I/O access checks. If IOPMP entry validation were routed through this path without HWCFG2.chk_x verification, I/O agent instruction-fetch violations would be silently misclassified as legal CPU-side accesses. The invariant 'this function is only called for CPU HART access checks' is not enforced anywhere in code.
  [RISK] include/sbi/sbi_domain.h:22-28  N/A (enum sbi_domain_access)
      The access taxonomy defines READ/WRITE/EXECUTE/MMIO, inherited from the CPU-side PMP model. It has no SRCID field to identify I/O agents, no distinct I/O-agent read/write/execute bits (which IOPMP ENTRY_CFG.sire/siwe/sere/sewe provide), and no per-entry error suppression mask. Software using this enum to configure IOPMP entries would implicitly treat all I/O agents identically to CPU HARTs, losing the ability to independently control I/O agent instruction-fetch permission — the very capability that HWCFG2.chk_x gates. The domain abstraction is CPU-centric by design, but nothing in the type system prevents its reuse for I/O agent contexts.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_protection.c:21-42  sbi_hart_protection_register
      Registration uses rating-based priority insertion into hart_protection_list with no capability verification. An IOPMP-backed sbi_hart_protection registered with a high rating would be selected by sbi_hart_protection_best() over PMP without any HWCFG2.chk_x check. This means on hardware where the IOPMP implementation lacks the instruction-fetch enforcement extension (chk_x=0 in HWCFG2), the 'best' protection mechanism would silently provide weaker enforcement than the PMP it displaced. The assumption 'higher rating implies strictly stronger protection' is not programmatically verified against hardware capability registers.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_mpxy.c:173-215  mpxy_std_attrs_init
      This function demonstrates the CORRECT capability-gating pattern: it checks for the presence of function pointers (send_message_with_response, send_message_without_response, get_notification_events, switch_eventsstate) and conditionally sets capability bits via CAP_ENABLE only when the corresponding callbacks are populated. This is the exact pattern an IOPMP capability table should replicate for HWCFG2 bits (chk_x, peis, pees, non_prio_en). The codebase already knows how to do capability gating — it just hasn't applied the pattern to I/O memory protection. This function itself is compliant; the gap is organizational, not a code defect.
  [RISK] platform/generic/sifive/sifive_dev_platform.c:17-42  sifive_smepmp_setup
      This function configures I/O access control through wgChecker — a platform-specific SiFive hardware block — rather than through the RISC-V IOPMP standard. The code itself is correct (it checks SBI_HART_EXT_SMEPMP before proceeding, then adds MMIO regions to the root domain). However, it demonstrates platform fragmentation: the codebase delegates I/O protection to vendor-specific hardware (wgChecker) while the RISC-V spec defines IOPMP as the standard mechanism. Software written to assume IOPMP-based I/O protection will not interoperate with wgChecker-enforced platforms. The implicit invariant 'I/O protection is the platform layer's responsibility' means domain-level code never sees which mechanism is actually enforcing I/O isolation — and thus cannot verify HWCFG2 capability bits for whatever mechanism is in use.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_init.c:320-331  init_coldboot
      The cold boot sequence calls sbi_mpxy_init() then sbi_domain_finalize() in that order. There is no IOPMP initialization step and no HWCFG2 capability register read anywhere in the init sequence. The comment documents that domain finalization must happen before HART PMP configuration so the correct domain is used — establishing a clear integration point. If a future IOPMP initialization were inserted at this location (after mpxy_init, before domain_finalize) without a preceding HWCFG2 capability read, the init code would bake in pre-reorg baseline assumptions about IOPMP features. The risk is architectural: the init sequence has no reserved slot for capability discovery, and the order-of-operations constraint ('domains finalized before PMP config') would naturally push an IOPMP init into a position where capability checks are easily omitted.

