      CSR_PMPCFG0–CSR_PMPCFG3 and CSR_PMPADDR0–CSR_PMPADDR15 are defined as bare address constants with no permission-bit macros (R/W/X/L/TOR) anywhere in the file. The file establishes a pattern of CSR-address-only definitions without encoding permission semantics. An IOPMP driver added here would naturally omit ENTRY_CFG.x, sire/siwe/sere/sewe field definitions, and HWCFG2 capability masks, meaning the driver would skip capability checks (chk_x, peis, pees, non_prio_en) and rely on absent hardware enforcement. This is a design-pattern risk, not a current code bug: no IOPMP driver exists yet, but the existing PMP pattern makes it likely a future driver would mirror the omission.Wrote 3 report(s) → output/riscv-iopmp/pr-148/

  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/encoding.h:1081-1100  (global scope)
      DECLARE_CSR invocations exist for pmpcfg0–pmpaddr15 (CPU PMP registers) but no IOPMP CSR declarations exist (HWCFG2, ENTRY_CFG, ERR_INFO). The DECLARE_CSR macro is conditionally defined by includers (e.g., db_disasm.c uses it for CSR-name-to-string mapping); for general kernel code, csr_read/csr_write use the symbolic name directly via inline assembly. An IOPMP driver would lack generated accessors for checking chk_x, peis/pees capability bits. Again a pattern risk: no IOPMP driver exists yet, so no current code actually reads HWCFG2.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/machdep.c:541-606  initriscv / efi_exclude_sbi_pmp_cb
      efi_exclude_sbi_pmp_cb (541-555) excludes OpenSBI PMP-protected firmware regions from phys_avail[], assuming the CPU PMP is the only memory-protection entity. The comment at 595-606 explicitly documents the kernel's trust that 'OpenSBI uses the first PMP entry to prevent buggy supervisor software from overwriting the firmware'. This pattern delegates all memory protection entirely to firmware with zero kernel-side PMP/IOPMP configuration. If running on hardware with an IOPMP that does NOT implement instruction-fetch checking (chk_x=0) or error suppression, the kernel neither checks HWCFG2 capability bits nor configures IOPMP entries — trusting firmware to provide full I/O enforcement that the hardware may not support. Currently safe on platforms with comprehensive firmware PMP setup, but the invariant 'firmware fully configures all I/O memory protection' is not checked in code.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:333-340  cpu_exception_handler (do_trap_supervisor)
      Panics unconditionally on SCAUSE_INST_ACCESS_FAULT (scause=1), treating it as an unrecoverable CPU PMP violation. No path exists for discriminating an IOPMP-generated error type 0x03 ('illegal instruction fetch') from an I/O agent — no ERR_INFO register read, no I/O agent fault discrimination, and no awareness of per-entry error suppression bits (sire/siwe/sere/sewe). On a system where the IOPMP does enforce instruction-fetch checking after proper configuration, an I/O agent fetch from a non-executable DMA buffer would trigger a CPU-level access fault indistinguishable from a genuine CPU PMP violation. Conversely, on a system where the IOPMP implementation does NOT support instruction-fetch checking (chk_x=0), the absence of checking means a malicious I/O agent's code-fetch from a DMA buffer goes entirely undetected. The current unconditional panic is 'safe' in that it halts the system, but it provides no diagnostic differentiation for IOPMP-origin faults.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/busdma_bounce.c:129-164  bounce_bus_dma_tag_create
      Creates busdma tags with bounce-flags only (BF_COHERENT, BF_COULD_BOUNCE, BF_MIN_ALLOC_COMP, BF_KMEM_ALLOC) — no execute-permission tracking. No BUS_DMA_NOEXEC flag exists to thread through tag_create and propagate to IOPMP entry configuration. The struct bus_dma_tag (line 71-77) carries bounce_zone and bounce_flags but no IOPMP-entry reference or execute-permission field. If an IOPMP driver were integrated, DMA buffers allocated through this path would be mapped with permissive IOPMP entries allowing instruction fetch by I/O agents, absent an explicit new flag and plumbing.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/busdma_machdep.c:114-139  bus_dma_tag_create
      Public entry point for DMA tag creation dispatching to bounce_bus_dma_tag_create. The flags parameter accepts BUS_DMA_* flags from the MI bus_dma.h interface — these are physical-address-constraint flags (alignment, boundary, lowaddr, highaddr) with no I/O-agent execute-permission semantics. This is the integration point where an IOPMP-aware busdma would need to accept per-entry permission flags mapping to ENTRY_CFG fields (ENTRY_CFG.x for execute, sire/siwe/sere/sewe for error suppression). Currently, the entire busdma layer has no concept of IOPMP entry permissions.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/bus_dma_impl.h:32-75  (struct definitions)
      struct bus_dma_tag_common (line 32) has alignment, boundary, lowaddr, highaddr, maxsize, nsegments, maxsegsz, flags, lockfunc, lockfuncarg — all physical-address-constraint or locking fields. No IOPMP entry pointer, no execute-permission flag, no error-suppression mask. struct bus_dma_impl (line 46) callbacks pass only physical-address constraints. An IOPMP-aware busdma would need either new fields on these structures or a parallel IOPMP-specific tag variant. The current design has no extension point for I/O memory protection attributes.
  [RISK] sys/contrib/device-tree/include/dt-bindings/clock/thead,th1520-clk-ap.h:120-125  (global scope)
      Defines CLK_IOPMP_VOSYS_DPU_PCLK through CLK_IOPMP_GPU_ACLK for the T-Head TH1520 SoC, proving this hardware has IOPMP blocks policing DPU/GPU DMA traffic. The clock enable names reveal high-bandwidth multimedia I/O agents (DPU, GPU) whose DMA should be policed by IOPMP. FreeBSD has no driver to configure these IOPMP blocks. The clocks exist in the DT binding layer but no kernel code ever enables them or configures the associated IOPMP entries. Any DMA from DPU/GPU proceeds through unconfigured (possibly permissive-reset-state) IOPMP entries.
  [RISK] sys/contrib/device-tree/Bindings/iommu/riscv,iommu.yaml:1-77  (binding schema)
      RISC-V IOMMU device tree binding schema describes page-level 'access and protection attributes' at IOMMU page-table granularity — a fundamentally different protection model from IOPMP entries. No IOPMP-specific properties exist: no iopmp-cells, no hwcfg2 capability nodes, no per-entry configuration nodes. If an IOPMP driver were added with device tree probing, it would find no existing binding to discover HWCFG2 capabilities (chk_x, peis, pees, non_prio_en) through the DT. This is an integration gap between the DT binding ecosystem and the IOPMP reorganized spec's capability discovery model.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/sbi.c:289-347  sbi_init
      Probes SBI extensions TIME, IPI, RFENCE, HSM, SRST (and legacy equivalents) and asserts on missing mandatory console/timer/IPI/fence extensions. No IOPMP SBI extension exists or is probed. If the IOPMP spec defined an SBI extension for firmware-managed IOPMP configuration (as it does for PMP via the HSM/legacy model), this init path would gate all I/O memory protection behind an unprobed extension, leaving IOPMP entries in their hardware-reset state. Currently compliant because no such SBI extension is standardized and no IOPMP driver exists to depend on it — but the extension-probing pattern here is the natural place where such a dependency would be added.


